From: gt510 <gt510@localhost>
Subject: media: venus: venc: rate control from the frame rate on HFI 1.x

The HFI 1.x firmware (Venus 1.8 on MSM8916) budgets bits per frame from
the input buffer timestamps unless told otherwise. The driver passes the
V4L2 timestamps through, but those are opaque cookies to a stateful
encoder: GStreamer's v4l2 encoders, for one, set them to the frame
number in seconds. The firmware then sees one frame per second and gives
each frame the budget of a whole second: on SM-T550 (640x480, 2 Mbit/s
VBR target) GStreamer got 7.4 Mbit/s on test bars and 25 Mbit/s on noise,
and Snapshot recordings ran ~10x over the bitrate. v4l2-ctl, which queues
real monotonic timestamps faster than real time, undershot instead.
Neither S_PARM, the H.264 level nor VUI timing changed the result.

Send HFI_PROPERTY_PARAM_VENC_DISABLE_RC_TIMESTAMP (already handled by
the 1.x packet builder) so that the firmware uses the frame rate set
through S_PARM, as the V4L2 encoder interface intends. With it the same
GStreamer encodes hit their targets: bars 2.00 Mbit/s (2 Mbit/s target),
noise 1.95 (CBR 2), 8.14 (VBR 8), 2.01 over 20 s (VBR 2), 1152x864 bars
4.00 (4).

Only tested on HFI 1.x; newer firmware may honour timestamps too.
---
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/venc.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/venc.c
@@ -851,6 +851,20 @@
 	ret = hfi_session_set_property(inst, ptype, &rate_control);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * Budget the bits from the frame rate (S_PARM), not from the buffer
+	 * timestamps: they are opaque cookies to the driver, and e.g.
+	 * GStreamer's v4l2 encoders use frame numbers in seconds, which made
+	 * the HFI 1.x firmware overshoot the target bitrate 4-14 times.
+	 */
+	if (IS_V1(inst->core)) {
+		ptype = HFI_PROPERTY_PARAM_VENC_DISABLE_RC_TIMESTAMP;
+		en.enable = 1;
+		ret = hfi_session_set_property(inst, ptype, &en);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
 
 	if (rate_control == HFI_RATE_CONTROL_CQ && ctr->const_quality) {
 		struct hfi_heic_frame_quality quality = {};
